data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"d62a8a18c6a59067a1137613c7f7fbe9c84c3636100eeeeb0791b77f0b64ee9d\",\"at\":1795707103320,\"retried\":[5],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Neil Armstrong (Commander), Michael Collins (Command Module Pilot), and Buzz Aldrin (Lunar Module Pilot), all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong became the first human to walk on the Moon about six hours after landing; Aldrin joined him nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The crew spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"After over 21 hours on the surface, they rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War geopolitical competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Uncrewed Soviet probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rockets so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was identified as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be highly impressive and important for space exploration, but very difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, building larger alternate boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Urgent National Needs\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"The speech says it will be an entire nation, not one man, going to the Moon, since all must work to put him there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy states there is no strife, prejudice, or national conflict in outer space yet, and its hazards are hostile to all.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy asks why the Moon is chosen as the goal, comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"The refrain 'We choose to go to the Moon' closes the excerpt, affirming the national commitment to the lunar goal.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Rice\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"outer space\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the goal is hard, to organize and measure the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The Moon landing effort already had the name Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961 Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Nikita Khrushchev, who declined; Kennedy proposed a joint expedition again in 1963 at the UN.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"The joint Moon mission idea was ultimately abandoned after Kennedy's death; an early key decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Kennedy\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo spacecraft.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo had three major parts: the command module, which housed three astronauts and was the only part returning to Earth; the service module, which provided propulsion, power, oxygen, and water; and the lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"This design meant the spacecraft could be launched by a single Saturn V rocket that was then under development.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Technologies and techniques required for Apollo were developed by Project Gemini, and Apollo used MOSFETs in the IMP and silicon IC chips in the AGC.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 in October 1968 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 in December 1968 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 in March 1969 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit, and Apollo 10 in May 1969 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to take the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Urgent National Needs\",\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University football stadium\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Rice\",\"Texas\",\"the Moon\",\"outer space\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"c2de7020ddd7dae4f53c53496d7273cc11f305b152bf2c9d095242c17340d9b3"}}

